        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best treadmill for heavy runners?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The Spirit XT685: a 425 lb maximum user weight on Spirit’s spec sheet, a 22 by 60-inch belt, a 4.0 HP motor, 3-inch rollers and a lifetime frame, motor and deck warranty, for about $2,899 on Amazon on 24 November 2026. If it has to fold, the BowFlex T16 (375 lb, 22 x 60 in., 12 mph) is the pick.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What weight capacity do I need to run on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Aim for 75 to 100 lb above your body weight if you run regularly, because each footstrike loads the deck with roughly two to three times body weight. A 250 lb runner should look for 325 to 350 lb; a 300 lb runner for 375 to 400 lb. Walkers can usually manage with about 50 lb of margin.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can a 300 lb person run on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, on a treadmill rated for it. Look for a capacity of 375 to 400 lb or more, a 60-inch belt, a top speed of at least 10 mph and a long motor warranty. If you are new to running at that weight, many people start with incline walking and walk-run intervals, and it is worth checking with your doctor before starting a new running plan.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a 350 lb treadmill enough for a 300 lb runner?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is at the limit. A 350 lb rating leaves only 50 lb of margin, which is fine for walking but tight for regular running. A 300 lb runner is better served by a 400 lb or 425 lb rating, such as the 3G Cardio Elite Runner X, Sole ST90 or Spirit XT685.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What motor size do heavy runners need?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The usual guidance is 3.0 CHP or more for regular running and 3.5 CHP or more for heavier runners. Few Amazon listings state whether their figure is continuous or peak, so weigh the motor warranty heavily: a lifetime motor warranty, as Spirit and BowFlex state, says more than a horsepower number.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are folding treadmills OK for heavy runners?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Some are, if the frame is rated well above your weight. The BowFlex T16 and SOLE F85 both fold and carry 375 lb ratings. Non-folding frames have fewer joints and are the safer choice for runners over about 300 lb.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
